A||||14||deceler8||Question re: laptop hard drives...||||1||
Z||000000||deceler8||06-22-2001||06:04 PM||||Funny how I've gotten this reputation for being able to fix computers...when all I know is what I learn from this forum !<P>But here's another problem.<P>I've been fixing up all my relatives with used PC's so they can all stay in touch via E-Mail. One of these computers is a 486 laptop with a puny 210MB hard drive. I have some old 340 hard drives and I wanted to put one in this laptop for the additional space.<P>Now for the problem. I stuck this in my desktop to copy some files over to it and while my computer auto-detects it (set up as secondary master), it doesn't assign a drive letter to it. Neither of my 340 meg drives (or the 210 meg that I know is working) are assigned drive letters, but all are auto-detected.<P>Does this mean these drives are bad ? I know the 210 is a working drive as it boots and has Win95 on it. The 340's someone gave to me and I've never used them.<P>My desktop is a Pentium 166.<P>How about helping this so-called computer guru once again ? And as usual, a thousand thank-you's.||||reg||1
Z||000001||Cliff Pennock||06-22-2001||06:23 PM||||Depends on the age of the HDs. Some very old HDs used a sector translation table (to get above a certain amount of MBs) and would only work if you booted your system from it. On most of these HDs you would see a message appear thta indicated the drive was using some sort of "large drive" support. Remember, many years ago a 210Mb HD was considered <B>huge</B>.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> Heck, I can remember I had a 80Mb HD in my system and people were asking me why in the world I would need such a large HD.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000002||84Bill||06-22-2001||06:36 PM||||OOOOh OOOHh I know!<BR>The drive needs to be the PRIMARY DRIVE on either the primary (boot) controller or the secondary controller. If you cant see the drive then from a dos boot disk run the FDISK and create a new partition.<BR>THE best way to partition the drive is to plug it into the primary controller and disconnect all other drives. This way you won't accidentaly partition your good drive.<BR>You are learning well young Grasshopper    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>I think I still have an old 20meg MFM laying arround somewhere  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><p>[This message has been edited by 84Bill (edited 06-22-2001).]||||reg||14
Z||000003||DecadenceR||06-22-2001||10:01 PM||||You also need to look at how many other devices are on the same IDE cable. If you have a hard drive on the same cable as the one you're adding, a lot of computers need you to use a jumper to identify the one drive as a master, and one as a slave.||||reg||1
Z||000004||deceler8||06-23-2001||09:41 AM||||I had them plugged into the secondary IDE slot as secondary master with no other drives on the cable.<P>They spin up and you can hear them working...so you're saying I should plug them into the primary IDE cable as master and go from there ?<P>And how do I create a DOS boot disk ? I have a Windows 95 boot disk...is this the same thing ?<P>Remember...we're dealing with a novice here (me), so I need specific instructions.<P>And thanks again for any help.||||reg||1
Z||000005||84Bill||06-23-2001||11:01 AM||||No just hook up 1 laptop drive to the decondary controller.||||reg||14
Z||000006||84Bill||06-23-2001||11:27 AM||||::deep breath::<P>Ok here is what we got<P><B>YOUR PC</B><P>1 You have a <B>PC</B> with a  (1) drive in it<P>2 The PC has 1 drive plugged into the master controller. It is the "C" (boot drive)that has windows and all your files that you need to get the PC to work. It is the "Primary Master drive" and is configurable to be either a "Primary Master drive" or "Secondary Slave drive" and can be plugged into the Primary Controller OR Secondary Controller<P>3 The PC has 2 controllers that can have 2 drives each attached to them. 2 drives on the "Primary controller" 2 drives on the "Secondary conteoller" for a total of 4 drives in your PC. and they are as follows<P>1 Primary controller<BR>   a Primary / Master (boot drive)<BR>   b Secondary / Slave<BR>2 Secondary Controller<BR>   a Primary / Master<BR>   b Secondary / Slave<P><B>LAPTOP</B><BR>1 A laptop drive is very diferent from a PC drive. It will ONLY work as a "Primary / Master drive" Since Laptops only have 1 controller and 1 drive in them. laptop hardrives to the best of my knowledge are not configurable IE can only be a Primary / Master.<P>If you want to access a LAPTOP drive on a PC you MUST plug the LAPTOP dirve into the SECONDARY CONTROLLER all by it's lonesome with no other drives attached to the cable as follows<P>1 Primary Controller<BR>  a PC Drive (boot drive)<BR>  b NONE<BR>2 Secondary Controller<BR>  a Laptop Drive<BR>  b NONE<P>With the above configuration you can boot to windows and access the Laptop Drive.<P>THIS WILL NOT WORK<BR>1 Primary Controller<BR>  a PC Drive (boot drive)<BR>  b Lptop Drive<BR>2 Secondary Controller<BR>  a NONE<BR>  b NONE<BR>With the above configuration you can boot to windows and will NOT be able to access the laptop drive.<P><BR>If you get the computer up and running with the laptop drive installed let me know and I will give you directions on what to do.<P>BTW 95 needs a boatload of space and on a 486 laptop it's just to slow to be worth wile to do.<BR><p>[This message has been edited by 84Bill (edited 06-23-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000007||deceler8||06-23-2001||12:59 PM||||That's what I did, Bill...my primary (C <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> is the primary master, my CDROM is also attached to the primary master (as slave) and the laptop drive is connected to the secondary as master (no other drives connected to the secondary).<P>It auto-detects the drives, but won't assign a drive letter to them.<P>I did this a couple of weeks ago with a 1.2 gig drive from another laptop and it did assign a drive letter and I was able to copy files to this drive.<P>Is the fact that the system won't assign a drive letter to the 340 meg drives an indication that they're bad ? And if they are, is there any reason I can't just get another 800meg or 1.2 gig drive and put it in this 486 laptop ?<P>The 210 meg drive from the laptop in question has Windows 95 on it and it does work in the laptop...I'm looking to add a little more space so I can put a newer version of Internet Explorer on the laptop.||||reg||1
Z||000008||deceler8||06-23-2001||01:02 PM||||Also, how do I make a DOS boot disk...is a Windows boot disk the same thing ?<P>Thanks again.||||reg||1
Z||000009||84Bill||06-23-2001||04:04 PM||||Okidoki<BR>when windows is booting up when it says "Starting windows" press the F8 key. A menu will appear, select "Command Prompt only" I think is #6. at the c:\ prompt type "FDISK" <P>A menu will appear select #5 Change drive letter. Select the D drive or dive #2. It should give you the size of the drive and all that bla bla. <P><BR>BE SURE YOU SELECT THE D DRIVE BEFORE YOU CONTINUE!!!!<P>The menu will jump back to the main. select <BR>#4 to see if you have the proper drive. This will give you info on the D Drive and note what type of partition it is. It will be either DOS, NON DOS, LOGICAL and EXTENDED<P>3 "delete partition"<BR>Now things get really hard to explain but depending on what that drive had on it you can usualy select one that matches what you noted as the partition type above.<P>After the partition has been deleted select #1 from the main menu "Create DOS partition" <BR>Creat a "Primary DOS Partition" and answer yes to all prompts. It will either drop you back out to DOS of tell you you need to reboot. <P>Reboot <BR>go back into windows and open "My Computer"<BR>you should see the drive now.<BR>It will need to be formated just "RIGHT" click on the drive and go to "format" and format the pupper<P>[This message has been edited by 84Bill (edited 06-23-2001).]<p>[This message has been edited by 84Bill (edited 06-23-2001).]||||reg||14
Z||000010||84Bill||06-24-2001||02:28 AM||||Hummm have not heard from him in a while. I wonder if the partition went ok. <A HREF="http://cn308598-a.wall1.pa.home.com/fiero/sounds/doh.wav" TARGET=_blank>DOH!!!</A>||||reg||5
Z||000011||Sootah||06-24-2001||02:38 AM||||Ahh. I'm so glad I'm not the only nerd here.<P>------------<BR>Gravity is a myth, the earth sucks||||reg||7
Z||000012||deceler8||06-24-2001||09:52 AM||||Hey Bill...went without a hitch. Worked like a charm on both drives.<P>But alas...another question. Is there a way to hook an external keyboard to a laptop other than the keyboard/mouse port ?<P>This piece of crap laptop I'm working on (really considering running over it with my car or blowing it up with firecrackers) has a defective external keyboard port and a failing internal keyboard. Can an external keyboard be hooked up through the serial port or whatever ?||||reg||1
Z||000013||84Bill||06-24-2001||11:17 AM||||keyboard/mouse is about the only way I know of besides USB and I know that thing doesn't have a USB port. The only way to fix it is to replace the motherboard ||||reg||14
Z||000014||DecadenceR||06-24-2001||01:22 PM||||I dunno about your laptop, but my old one had a port specifically for a keyboard, and another specifically for the mouse. But otherwise, exactly what 84Bill said.||||reg||1
